# Copy to `local.properties` and fill in. That file is gitignored — never commit it,
# and never put a real value in this template.
#
# Everything here is optional. With none of it set the sample still builds and runs —
# the map screens say a key is missing rather than showing a blank map.
#
# This file is now the SMALLER half of the build's configuration. Shared, non-credential
# values live in the committed `configuration.properties` at the repository root, so a
# fresh clone builds against the right endpoints without copying anything by hand.
#
# What still belongs HERE:
#   * credentials — MAPS_API_KEY, TRACKER_LICENSE. Nothing reads these from the committed
#     file, deliberately: a hurried paste there is a live credential published to everyone
#     with repo access, in every clone, recoverable from history after the line is deleted.
#   * per-machine overrides of SYNC_URL and OSRM_BASE_URL. A value here wins; blank or
#     absent falls through to `configuration.properties`.
#
# What no longer belongs here at all: FIELDTRACK_LICENSE_URL and FIELDTRACK_RESPONSE_KEY.
# Those are product configuration, read only from `configuration.properties` (or `-P…` /
# an environment variable for one run). A line for either below does nothing.

# Android SDK location. Android Studio writes this for you on first sync.
#sdk.dir=/path/to/Android/sdk

# Google Maps key for the Track and Debug screens.
# Restrict it to this app's package + signing certificate in Google Cloud Console;
# an unrestricted key is billable by anyone who finds it.
#MAPS_API_KEY=

# OSRM base URL for road snapping. Leave unset unless you run your own instance.
#
# Unset is a working configuration: no RoadSnapProvider is installed, buildTrack never
# leaves the device, and the track renders from raw fixes. Setting it makes the polyline
# follow the road network instead of the fixes.
#
# Point this at a deployment you control. The public demo server documents no availability
# guarantee and no rate limit worth relying on, and sending a fleet's traces to it is both
# unreliable and somebody else's bill.
#OSRM_BASE_URL=https://osrm.internal.example.com

# Upload endpoint for TrackerSync — the FULL URL the batch is POSTed to, not a base.
#
# Unset is a working configuration and the default: the sample configures no upload half,
# points accumulate in Room, and nothing opens a socket. That is the offline-first
# behaviour the SDK is built around, so the sample has to keep demonstrating it.
#
# Set it and the sample turns on autoSync — points upload as they are accepted, a backlog
# recorded offline drains when connectivity returns, and the Home screen shows how many
# rows are still queued.
#
# Not a credential, so the usual warning does not apply — but it is per-developer. A dev
# tunnel URL is ephemeral and points at one laptop, which is why there is no default.
#SYNC_URL=https://api.example.com/locations

# FieldTrack licence API root, and the key that verifies /verify RESPONSES.
#
# NOT READ FROM THIS FILE. Both are product configuration and live in the committed
# `configuration.properties` — one endpoint and one public verification key that the whole
# team and every CI runner build against, rather than a per-machine value that lets one
# laptop ship a build aimed somewhere nobody tested. Neither is a credential: both are
# compiled into BuildConfig and readable in any published AAR or installed APK either way.
#
# To point one build elsewhere for one run, pass -PfieldtrackLicenseUrl=... /
# -PfieldtrackResponseKey=..., or set FIELDTRACK_LICENSE_URL / FIELDTRACK_RESPONSE_KEY in
# the environment. Both beat the committed file. Editing them here does nothing.
